//! WHERE A SEAT REFUSED WHAT IT WAS HANDED, as the program layer's typing found it: a node whose
//! type can't stand in the seat that wants it, in every body a written item holds — a fn's or a
//! method's, a seat's or a field's default, a const's value — and in the statements an entry runs.
//! The same walk keeps every name those bodies read that nothing answers.
